The invention concerns a technique for automatically supporting the landing of an aircraft on a runway, from a return point (A) to an end point (D) where the aircraft makes contact with the runway. This assistance is carried out by a data-processing unit installed on board the aircraft, the unit being arranged for connection to an altimeter and to a deviation meter. The method comprises:

- a return-navigation assistance stage, in which the aircraft is guided from the return point (A) toward the location of the deviation meter (E). This guidance uses measurements, supplied by the deviation meter, of the aircraft’s azimuth offset relative to a reference direction extending between the return point (A) and the deviation meter position (E). During this stage, the aircraft’s position is established at a predefined capture point (B), which lies in alignment with the return point (A) and the deviation meter position (E). The aircraft is then guided along a preset route from the capture point (B) to a predetermined holding point (C), which is substantially aligned with the runway axis, using altitude information from the altimeter together with aircraft heading and speed data;

- a landing assistance stage, in which the aircraft is guided from the holding point (C) to the completion point (D).

